# an operand that is a call, which is where an operand register is filled by an
# op that runs the call rather than by a read or by an inner operator. The call
# stands on each side of the operator in turn, and under every family: an op
# whose route always stepped into the left operand would land on `1` in
# `say 1 + length(za)`, which is not a call at all.
program
za = 'ab'
zb = 3
say length(za) + 1
say 1 + length(za)
say 'n=' || length(za)
say length(za) = 2
say length(za) > 1 & zb > 1
say length(za) length(za)
say zb || length(za) || zb
----
rc> 0
out> 3
out> 3
out> n=2
out> 1
out> 1
out> 2 2
out> 323

# a call under a prefix operator, which is the one-operand case of the stanza
# above. `- -length(za)` puts the call two steps down with neither step the
# one a binary operator's right operand takes, so a route that spelled a
# prefix's only operand the way it spells a right operand finds nothing there.
program
za = 'abc'
say -length(za)
say +length(za)
say \(length(za) = 3)
say - -length(za)
say -length(za) + 1
----
rc> 0
out> -3
out> 3
out> 0
out> 3
out> -2

# two calls in one expression, and a call inside another call's argument. **The
# two calls are of different routines**, which is what says each op resolves a
# name of its own: measured, handing every call op in a chunk the same
# resolution site reddens this stanza, where a stanza whose calls were all
# `length` would pass under exactly that mutation. They stand in both orders so
# that neither position is the only one covered.
# The arguments are the other half. A call's arguments are not compiled, so the
# `substr` in `length(substr(za, 2))` and the `||` in the row below it are
# evaluated by the argument loop and by `eval.rs`, with the outer call the only
# node here holding an op.
program
za = 'abcd'
say length(za) + length(za)
say length(za) || pos('c', za)
say pos('c', za) || length(za)
say length(substr(za, 2))
say length(substr(za, 2) || 'xy')
say max(length(za), length(substr(za, 2)))
----
rc> 0
out> 8
out> 43
out> 34
out> 3
out> 5
out> 4

# a failure reached through a call in a nested position, trapped, so that one
# row says what is reported and which clause SIGL lands on for each place the
# failure comes from: the divide inside the call's own argument list gives 42,
# the argument the callee wanted and did not get gives 40, and the operator
# converting what the call answered gives 41. SIGL is the whole `SAY`'s clause
# in every one, which is the same answer this file's other trapped stanza
# records for an operand that is not a call.
program
signal on syntax name z1
say 1 + length(1/0)
z1:
say 'in-arg' rc 'at' sigl
signal on syntax name z2
say 'q' + length('ab')
z2:
say 'operator' rc 'at' sigl
signal on syntax name z3
say 1 + substr('abc')
z3:
say 'callee' rc 'at' sigl
----
rc> 0
out> in-arg 42 at 2
out> operator 41 at 6
out> callee 40 at 10

